  :adr/body "2026-09-01. x402 client libraries for TypeScript and Python, at
kotoba-lang/x402-sdk, with the signer injected.

## Why an SDK at all

x402 is four HTTP steps: ask, read the 402, pay, ask again with the proof. It
is small enough that a library's job is not to hide it. What it is for is the
handful of details that are easy to get wrong and expensive when they are.

## The four the SDK exists for

**The EIP-712 domain comes from the offer.** Base mainnet USDC declares
`USD Coin`; Base Sepolia USDC declares `USDC`. Signing under the wrong one
produces a signature that recovers a different address, and the payment is
rejected as a bad signature -- which reads like the buyer's fault. The client
refuses an offer carrying no domain rather than guessing one, and raises on an
unknown network rather than defaulting its chain id.

**The cheapest allowed offer wins, never accepts[0].** Sellers list the same
resource on mainnet and on a testnet in their own order. Taking the first is
how a buyer pays real money to rehearse.

**A policy is required.** No policy refuses with `no-policy` rather than
paying. A default ceiling is a limit nobody chose.

**Every failing check is named, not just the first.** An offer on the wrong
network usually names that network's token too, so a caller told only
`network-not-allowed` fixes the network and is refused again.

## The signer is injected

These packages hold no key, sign nothing, and have no cryptography dependency.
The wallet stays wherever its owner put it: viem, ethers, eth-account, a
hardware device, a remote signer. A payment library that wants a key is a
place a key can leak from, and this is the same injection seam the rest of
this workspace uses -- webauthn's verifier, base-l2's transport, kotobase's
sign-fn.

It also keeps the packages honest about scope. They implement `exact`, where
the facilitator submits and pays the gas so a buyer needs no ETH. An offer
asking for `transaction` is refused by name rather than half-supported.

## Two defects the tests did not find

Both came from running against the live facilitator instead of against the
suite.

urllib's default User-Agent gets 403 from Cloudflare, so a Python client using
it cannot reach x402.nexus at all -- and the failure arrives looking like
authorisation rather than like a bot rule. The default fetch now sends a
User-Agent.

`select_offer` handed a None challenge raised AttributeError, reporting a
protocol outcome as a bug in the caller's own code. It refuses with
`not-a-challenge`.

## Languages

TypeScript and Python, following the layout of net-kotobase/sdk
(packages/<language>, native tooling per package, one wire contract in
spec/wire.md). Two done and exercised against a live service beats four
scaffolded. Rust and Go are the obvious next two; the contract they implement
is already written down.

The wire contract is transcribed from kotoba-lang/pay's pay.x402 -- what
x402.nexus actually runs -- rather than from a specification the server might
have drifted from.

## Not published to a registry

The code is public at kotoba-lang/x402-sdk and installable from git. Publishing
to npm and PyPI needs registry credentials this session does not have, so the
package manifests are ready and the publish step is not done. Saying they are
installable by name would be a claim about a registry nobody has pushed to."}]
